  <url type="homepage">https://github.com/snaketech-tu/kubri</url>
  <url type="bugtracker">https://github.com/snaketech-tu/kubri/issues</url>
  <launchable type="desktop-id">io.github.snaketech_tu.kubri.desktop</launchable>
  <content_rating type="oars-1.1"/>
  <categories>
    <category>Development</category>
  </categories>
  <keywords>
    <keyword>kubernetes</keyword>
    <keyword>k8s</keyword>
    <keyword>k9s</keyword>
    <keyword>kubectl</keyword>
  </keywords>
  <description>
    <p>
      Kubri shows every resource of your Kubernetes clusters live, with the columns of kubectl get, and covers what
      k9s can do: logs, shells, port-forwards, Helm, RBAC and the k9s keys, plugins and skins.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>An overview of every cluster in your kubeconfig, and a topology graph of how objects connect</li>
      <li>A log workspace with tabs, structured logs and debug containers</li>
      <li>Safe changes: context colors, read-only production contexts and a diff before every change</li>
      <li>What changed in a cluster, and snapshots to look at later</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In this Flatpak, Kubri reads ~/.kube and the k9s files in ~/.config/k9s. The sandbox cannot run commands
      installed on your computer, so kubeconfigs that sign in through one (such as aws or gke-gcloud-auth-plugin),
      k9s plugins and Helm changes need one of the other downloads on the release page.
    </p>
  </description>
